d arrest warrants in connection to  sessions murder before frank's body was  found prosecutors say eastman slit  session's throat in frank's apartment  and that eastman fatally shot frank in  the chest twice to silence the only  witness to sessions killing hiding her  body on the property of his new employer  troy bonnell investigators say eastman  burned sessions body and was allegedly  preparing to burn frank's body when he  was arrested at a nearby gas station  bonnell spent the entire day monday on  the stand eastman's defense attorney  said bonnell's inconsistent testimony  from previous video interviews proved  his lack of credibility as a witness  they pointed a finger at him for frank's  death after prosecutors specifically  asked if he was involved  when did you first learn of heather  frank's  deceased body being found on your  property  uh  it would have been hours after i was  at the  police station  did you have any involvement in heather  frank's death no on december 29th 2020  you learned that dna your dna was found  on this rank  i was told it was found on a pack of  cigarettes  that your dna was found in a pack of  cigarettes on her deceased body  i did not hear that  and you were asked uh at that point to  explain  um  why that why that was  yeah i don't recall hearing that i i  recalled ask being asked about a pack of  cigarettes that would why how would that  of my dna be on a pack of cigarettes  that was located on this drink i did not  hear the part of where she  where she was she had a pack of  cigarettes  on this on her on a deceased body  do you think bono had anything to do  with frank's murder and do you believe  it beyond a reasonable doubt  well look i think it's a smart move for  the defense to try to point to someone  else but i don't think that it has been  shown beyond a reasonable doubt that  bonnell had anything to do with that  murder yes it was on his property and  frankly that's not a good look but i  don't think that they've discovered or  shown or demonstrated in any way shape  or form that he had anything to do  with the killing or even with the  disposal of  the body so i i think that the  defense still has a very difficult case  and and the defense is going to have a  hard time showing that kevin eastman  wasn't the one who obviously killed both  uh of the victims and tried to you know  burn both of the bodies yeah maybe he's  not the other suspect but bernardo what  do you think of the defense's argument  that bunnell isn't credible and if he's  not how does that hurt the prosecution's  case  well in terms of every witness that  testifies for the prosecution  credibility is a huge role it's a huge  factor that the jury has to determine  whether this witness is actually telling  the truth so if you're able to crack  down on a person's credibility and the  jury finds some kind of reasonable doubt  because they can't believe this witness  then guess what whether you think he did  it didn't do it or had some kind of  inclination or gut feeling they did it  it doesn't matter because you have to  prove the defendant's guilt beyond a  reasonable doubt and that piece of doubt  right there will be enough to actually  acquit it yeah it looks like the defense  is getting two bites of the apple with  this theory as to this witness we'll see  how it plays out make sure to follow the  law and cry network for gavin gavel  coverage on the kevin dean eastman case  when we come back steve bannon is headed  to trial what to expect as the former  white house chief strategist stands  trial for contempt of congress  welcome back to law and crime daily a  federal judge orders the trial of former  white house chief strategist steve  bannon to move forward on charges of  contempt of congress  bannon defied a subpoena from the  january 6th committee last year to  testify and provide documents related to  his involvement in the efforts to  overturn the 2020 election when he  didn't appear by the deadline the  justice department charged bannon with  two counts of contempt of congress  bannon made a last-minute offer this  week to testify before the committee  days ahead of his criminal trial the  offer was accompanied by a letter from  former president donald trump claiming  to waive executive privilege over  bannon's testimony a move the committee  and justice department say may not be  applicable to a former president on  monday a judge denied bannon's request  to delay the trial and ruled nearly  every proposed defense from bannon's  attorney is a relevance to the alleged  crime against him his trial is scheduled  to begin next monday  bernard is bannon choosing to testify  days before his trial on a fence he  could use against these contempt charges  and will they work  brian stephen bannon sees it that the  writing is on the wall that he's going  to be convicted so i think what he's  doing by asking to testify or agreeing  to testify at this point he wants to  mitigate his losses in the sense that  once he is convicted he wants to do in  sentences let a judge know like look i  volunteered to speak in front of the  committee i volunteered to actually talk  at this time but it's too little too  late at this point he's facing one month  up to 12 months in custody and a fine  from a hundred dollars up to a hundred  thousand dollars so we'll wait and see  what's going to happen but i think it's  too little too late yeah and tyrion this  is a little aspect that i thought was  interesting as well why do you think  bannon attached a letter from former  president donald trump waving a  privilege many argued never existed  well he's trying to say that that  privilege did exist and that trump has  the right to waive the privilege and  trump is trying to claim the same thing  but i agree with bernard she says too  little too late i say too bad so sad  bannon is definitely going to either pay  a fine or go to jail and here's why  judge nichols basically dismissed all of  his defenses he not only said you're not  going to win a motion to dismiss but he  said you have no defense of privilege he  said you have no defense that the  process didn't work right so his only  defense is going to be that it wasn't  intentional and he's not going to be  able to show that because he completely  ignored that subpoena that was issued  back in september and even if he had  just responded and said you know just  postpone that might have helped but he  didn't do it sounds like he got bad  legal advice leading evidence well  bernardo terry thank you very much and  thank you for joining us here on law in  crime daily we'll see you next time as  we discuss justice in america  [Music]  you
 

